{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And one who steals a man and sells him.</b> The reason this verse enters between 'striking' and 'cursing,' as the Gaon Rabbeinu Saadya, of blessed memory, wrote, is that most of those stolen are children, and when they return after a long time they do not recognize father and mother, and they strike them and curse them — and so the kidnapper is the cause of striking and cursing, and therefore it is fitting that he be liable to death." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, wrote concerning this that the Torah placed 'stealing' next to 'striking' because their mode of execution is the same, namely strangulation. And therefore it needed to place 'and one who curses his father' after them, whose execution is by stoning, as it is written (Leviticus 20:9): ״אביו ואמו קלל דמיו בו״ — 'he has cursed his father and his mother; his blood is upon him' — and every place where it says 'his blood is upon him' refers only to stoning, as it is said (ibid.): ״באבן ירגמו אותם דמיהם בם״ — 'they shall be stoned with stones; their blood is upon them.' And the reason they were more severe with the one who curses than with the one who strikes — in that the Torah punished him with stoning, which is the severest of the four modes of execution of the court — is because it is more common for a person to curse his father and mother than to strike them, and an offense, according to its prevalence, always requires a greater punishment; or because there is in cursing a greater sin, namely the invocation of the Name, blessed be He — and so he must be punished for his sin against his father and mother and for bearing the Name of his Elohim for transgression and sin.' Thus far his words." "<b>One may also say</b> that the reason this verse enters here is to magnify the guilt of one who steals a person, and to make known the severity of the prohibition in it, for it is as severe as one who strikes and one who curses his father and mother; therefore it was placed in the middle between 'striking' and 'cursing.' And so too in the Ten Commandments, the Scripture placed it between 'adultery' and 'false testimony.' And had the Scripture mentioned it only at the end, after 'striking' and 'cursing,' it would have appeared that it is a lighter offense than striking and cursing; and it could not have placed it earlier, for it had already begun with the law of murder — 'one who strikes a man and he dies' — and it needed to place after it 'one who strikes his father and mother.' And indeed, on account of the greatness of this offense, the brothers were punished for it with the Ten Martyrs, and the matter of the doubled execution shall not be hidden from you, from what I have written elsewhere." "<b>And he is found in his hand.</b> This excludes one who is commonly in his hand, such as his son or his student, or one who regularly dines at his table — that he is not liable to death for them if he sold them."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:34.984538393Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}